  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nA fairly stacked low pressure system will push south southeast\nacross southern Canada and much of North Dakota tonight through\nSunday morning. The result will be increasing low clouds\ntonight, lingering through much of the day Sunday. This system\ncould also return areas of light snow as the low moves through.\nCanadian radar showing snow wrapping around the low currently in\nsouthern Saskatchewan. If this trend continues, the higher \nchances for snow will come across the north and east, closer to \nthe progressing low, although flurries will be possible with any\nlow clouds as this system moves through. Some light snow \naccumulations are also possible tonight through Sunday morning, \nespecially in the north central and northeast where NBM probs \nfor an inch or more are currently at 10 to 40%. Otherwise look \nfor a steady northwest wind tonight, breezy at times during \npassage of the low. Low temperatures will be in the teens and \n20s. Lingering light snow should push eastward Sunday morning, \nwith mostly cloudy skies, a few flurries, and cooler \ntemperatures in the mid 20s to mid 30s during the day. Breezy \nnorthwest winds will also continue for tomorrow, especially in \nthe east. Building surface high then moves in Sunday night \neroding away sky cover and diminishing winds. Depending on \ntiming of clearing skies, some patchy fog is possible although \noverall dry conditions should limit this threat. Cold lows will \nbe found as a result of these clearing skies with lows in the \nsingle digits to lower teens. Surface high then takes over for \nMonday with mainly clear skies and light winds. Highs will \ncontinue to be cool and in the mid 20s to mid 30s. Surface high \nmoves east Monday night, with a southerly flow returning across \nthe west. Some increased higher level clouds may also be found \nas a result, limiting fog threat. Temperatures will generally be\nin the teens.\n\nFor the upcoming week, clusters indicate weak ridging to the\nwest will create period of northwest flow. In this flow could be\nsome weak disturbance at times, which may result in low chances\nfor light snow. Although the moment NBM keeping a mainly dry\nforecast through the Thanksgiving week. With nearby ridging,\ntemperatures look to slightly warm through the week and return\nto near or perhaps slightly above normal. Thanksgiving weekend\nhas wider temperature spreads depending on how far inland the\nridge moves. The overall spreads indicate at least near normal\ntemperatures remain, with the possibility of mild temperatures.\nAny passing wave could also bring a period of breezy winds.\nCurrently the NBM is hinting this is possible on Friday.",
